            "round"
          ],
          "high": 28,
          "low": 8,
          "param": "data_width"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 30,
          "low": 4,
          "param": "n_iter"
        },
        {
          "choices": [
            "trunc",
            "round"
          ],
          "high": 8,
          "low": 2,
          "param": "m"
        }
      ],
      "why": "pipelined_m registers only every m stages, so for the same n_iter it uses fewer flip-flops than pipelined (ceil(N/m)+2 vs N+2 stages) at the cost of a longer combinational path (m micro-rotations per stage). Since the throughput floor is only 1 MSPS, the Fmax penalty from a deeper stage is acceptable, so pipelined_m can plausibly beat pipelined on area at equal accuracy. The m dimension is the key new trade-off: large m cuts register area but lowers Fmax and may push the stage too deep; small m approaches pipelined. I search the full m range (2..8) and the full accuracy dimensions so the front captures the register-depth vs path-depth trade-off that pipelined alone cannot express."
    }
  ],
  "rationale": "The spec is area-minimisation with a hard throughput floor of 1 MSPS and a hard accuracy floor of max_abs_err <= 2^-10. Both of my families produce 1 result/cycle, so throughput = Fmax and the 1 MSPS constraint is easily satisfied; the binding constraints are therefore accuracy (which fixes the minimum n_iter / guard combination) and area (which is driven by register count and datapath width). pipelined and pipelined_m are the only families that give 1 result/cycle without the shared-datapath/FSM overhead of iterative/unrolled_k, and they differ precisely in the register-count vs combinational-depth trade-off, which is the central area lever here. I give pipelined the larger share (0.55) because it is the simpler, more predictable low-area baseline and its front is the reference curve; pipelined_m (0.45) is the challenger that can undercut it on area via fewer registers. I keep the full registry ranges for data_width, n_iter, angle_guard, frac_guard and rounding in both families so that, once the feasible region is found, the reserved front-mapping pass can trace the complete trade-off curve from the area corner to the accuracy corner, maximising hypervolume. Rounding is left as a 2-choice dimension because it is a cheap accuracy/area knob that can shift the feasible boundary by a bit or two."
}

